1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,267
The twenty-fourth of September, 2014.

2
00:00:04,587 --> 00:00:08,206
Two hundred and twenty-four million kilometres from Earth,

3
00:00:08,206 --> 00:00:12,138
a spacecraft about as tall as a person is falling towards Mars.

4
00:00:12,458 --> 00:00:17,394
To be captured, it must fire its engine at exactly the right moment, and slow down.

5
00:00:17,714 --> 00:00:19,205
Nobody on Earth can help.

6
00:00:19,205 --> 00:00:22,785
A radio signal takes twelve and a half minutes to get there.

7
00:00:23,105 --> 00:00:25,819
If the burn fails, it flies past Mars, forever.

8
00:00:27,339 --> 00:00:27,999
It works.

9
00:00:28,319 --> 00:00:31,469
India becomes the first Asian country to reach Mars,

10
00:00:31,469 --> 00:00:35,226
and the first in the world to get there on its very first try.

11
00:00:35,546 --> 00:00:38,855
And the whole mission cost about seventy-four million dollars.

12
00:00:39,175 --> 00:00:41,328
That's less than the Hollywood film Gravity.

13
00:00:42,248 --> 00:00:43,491
So how did India do it?

14
00:00:48,733 --> 00:00:51,726
To see why this was remarkable, start with the problem.

15
00:00:52,066 --> 00:00:55,506
Earth and Mars both circle the Sun, at different speeds.

16
00:00:55,506 --> 00:00:58,208
So the distance between them keeps changing,

17
00:00:58,208 --> 00:01:02,631
from about fifty-five million kilometres to around four hundred million.

18
00:01:02,971 --> 00:01:07,659
You can't fly straight there. You launch onto a long, curving path around the Sun,

19
00:01:07,659 --> 00:01:10,232
and aim for where Mars will be, months later.

20
00:01:10,572 --> 00:01:15,258
Even on the best path, the trip takes many months. Mangalyaan's took about ten.

21
00:01:15,598 --> 00:01:20,389
The planets line up for this only once every twenty-six months. Miss the window,

22
00:01:20,389 --> 00:01:22,366
and you wait more than two years.

23
00:01:22,706 --> 00:01:26,224
And getting there is only half the battle. Over the decades,

24
00:01:26,224 --> 00:01:30,152
many missions to Mars have failed. Some on launch, some on the way,

25
00:01:30,152 --> 00:01:31,677
some in the final minutes.

26
00:01:32,017 --> 00:01:35,297
Until then, India had only ever been as far as the Moon.

27
00:01:39,137 --> 00:01:41,137
Even the space powers struggled at first.

28
00:01:41,477 --> 00:01:47,506
The Soviet Union's first two Mars probes, in 1960, never even reached orbit.

29
00:01:47,846 --> 00:01:51,652
America's first attempt, Mariner three, failed in 1964,

30
00:01:51,652 --> 00:01:54,420
when its protective cover wouldn't open.

31
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:59,249
Japan's Nozomi never managed to enter Mars orbit, in 2003.

32
00:01:59,249 --> 00:02:02,655
China's first Mars orbiter was lost in 2011,

33
00:02:02,655 --> 00:02:06,525
along with the Russian probe that was carrying it.

34
00:02:06,865 --> 00:02:09,956
Reaching Mars on the first try was almost unheard of.

35
00:02:13,796 --> 00:02:18,333
On the third of August, 2012, the Indian government approved the mission.

36
00:02:18,673 --> 00:02:22,351
The next launch window would open in November 2013.

37
00:02:22,691 --> 00:02:28,113
That left about fifteen months to build a spacecraft that could survive a journey to another planet.

38
00:02:31,953 --> 00:02:33,419
Trick one: a smaller rocket.

39
00:02:33,759 --> 00:02:40,030
ISRO's most powerful rocket at the time, the GSLV, had failed twice in 2010.

40
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:43,413
So they chose the smaller, reliable PSLV.

41
00:02:43,753 --> 00:02:48,401
On the fifth of November, 2013, it lifted off from Sriharikota.

42
00:02:48,741 --> 00:02:53,949
But the PSLV wasn't powerful enough to throw the spacecraft straight to Mars.

43
00:02:53,949 --> 00:02:57,872
It could only put it into a long, oval orbit around Earth.

44
00:02:58,212 --> 00:03:03,298
So ISRO used a clever trick. Every time the spacecraft swung closest to Earth,

45
00:03:03,298 --> 00:03:06,168
where it moves fastest, it fired its engine.

46
00:03:06,508 --> 00:03:08,938
Engineers call this the Oberth effect.

47
00:03:08,938 --> 00:03:13,414
A push given where you're already moving fastest adds the most energy.

48
00:03:13,754 --> 00:03:17,121
Each burn stretched the orbit wider. Over about a month,

49
00:03:17,121 --> 00:03:22,171
a series of burns pushed its highest point out beyond a hundred thousand kilometres.

50
00:03:22,511 --> 00:03:26,778
Then, on the first of December, one final burn flung it free of Earth,

51
00:03:26,778 --> 00:03:28,363
and onto the road to Mars.

52
00:03:32,203 --> 00:03:33,453
Trick two: keep it small.

53
00:03:33,793 --> 00:03:38,329
At launch it weighed about thirteen hundred and forty kilograms, and most of that,

54
00:03:38,329 --> 00:03:40,542
about eight hundred and fifty, was fuel.

55
00:03:40,882 --> 00:03:44,515
Mangalyaan carried just five science instruments. Together,

56
00:03:44,515 --> 00:03:46,794
they weighed about fifteen kilograms.

57
00:03:47,134 --> 00:03:51,184
A camera to photograph the planet. Sensors to study its atmosphere,

58
00:03:51,184 --> 00:03:52,635
and to hunt for methane.

59
00:03:52,975 --> 00:03:57,460
And its structure and engines were similar to India's first Moon probe,

60
00:03:57,460 --> 00:04:02,513
Chandrayaan-one. Proven designs meant less new engineering, and less to pay for.

61
00:04:06,353 --> 00:04:07,508
Trick three is people.

62
00:04:07,848 --> 00:04:11,203
A large share of any space mission's cost is salaries.

63
00:04:11,203 --> 00:04:16,732
And engineers in India earn a fraction of what they would in the United States or Europe.

64
00:04:17,072 --> 00:04:22,231
And nearly everything was designed and built in India, avoiding expensive imports.

65
00:04:22,571 --> 00:04:25,908
A small team, a tight schedule, and no time for extras.

66
00:04:29,748 --> 00:04:31,034
Not everything went to plan.

67
00:04:31,374 --> 00:04:36,390
On the eleventh of November, 2013, during the fourth orbit-raising burn,

68
00:04:36,390 --> 00:04:41,128
the main engine shut down early. A valve fault had cut off its fuel.

69
00:04:41,468 --> 00:04:44,864
Engineers planned an extra burn to make up the difference,

70
00:04:44,864 --> 00:04:47,030
and the spacecraft got back on track.

71
00:04:47,370 --> 00:04:52,693
Then came the long cruise. For about three hundred days, the main engine sat unused,

72
00:04:52,693 --> 00:04:54,341
in the cold of deep space.

73
00:04:54,681 --> 00:04:56,556
Would it fire again when it mattered most?

74
00:04:57,496 --> 00:05:03,178
On the twenty-second of September, 2014, two days before arrival, ISRO tested it,

75
00:05:03,178 --> 00:05:05,493
for just four seconds. It worked.

76
00:05:05,833 --> 00:05:10,179
Two days later came the burn you saw at the start. Twenty-four minutes,

77
00:05:10,179 --> 00:05:12,506
in Mars's shadow, entirely on its own.

78
00:05:16,346 --> 00:05:21,709
Mangalyaan wasn't alone. That same week, NASA's MAVEN orbiter also reached Mars.

79
00:05:22,049 --> 00:05:24,485
A bigger spacecraft, with more instruments,

80
00:05:24,485 --> 00:05:27,204
and a budget of several hundred million dollars.

81
00:05:27,544 --> 00:05:29,870
Both worked. One cost a fraction of the other.

82
00:05:33,710 --> 00:05:36,123
Mangalyaan was designed to last six months.

83
00:05:36,463 --> 00:05:38,333
It kept working for about eight years.

84
00:05:38,673 --> 00:05:41,739
It photographed the whole planet, and its moon, Phobos,

85
00:05:41,739 --> 00:05:43,690
and studied the Martian atmosphere.

86
00:05:44,030 --> 00:05:50,112
India was so proud of it that Mangalyaan later appeared on the back of the two-thousand-rupee banknote.

87
00:05:50,452 --> 00:05:56,691
Contact was finally lost in 2022, when its fuel ran out and its batteries drained.

88
00:05:57,031 --> 00:06:01,584
Spread over a journey of roughly six hundred and fifty million kilometres,

89
00:06:01,584 --> 00:06:06,506
Prime Minister Modi pointed out, the trip cost about seven rupees per kilometre.

90
00:06:06,846 --> 00:06:08,451
Less than an auto-rickshaw ride.

91
00:06:12,290 --> 00:06:13,718
So cheap didn't mean careless.

92
00:06:14,058 --> 00:06:16,621
It meant choosing simple, proven solutions,

93
00:06:16,621 --> 00:06:19,960
and letting physics do the work that money usually does.

94
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:21,323
Nine years later,

95
00:06:21,323 --> 00:06:26,257
the same frugal approach helped Chandrayaan-three land near the Moon's south pole,

96
00:06:26,257 --> 00:06:28,484
the first mission ever to land there.

97
00:06:28,824 --> 00:06:32,640
That's how India reached Mars for less than the cost of a movie,

98
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:34,786
and got there on its very first try.

99
00:06:39,426 --> 00:06:42,187
If you enjoyed this, subscribe to Noksa Webcasts,

100
00:06:42,187 --> 00:06:44,441
and tell me what we should explain next.
